menu "Firefly bring-up (S15 slice b)"

    choice FF_BRINGUP_STAGE
        prompt "Display/touch bring-up stage"
        default FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_1
        help
            S15 slice b is bring-up-in-the-loop: the maintainer flashes one
            stage, looks at the glass, then advances. Each stage is a strict
            superset of the previous one's init, so a dark screen at stage N
            still tells you exactly how far the earlier stages got from the
            serial log. Switch with `idf.py menuconfig` (this menu) or set
            CONFIG_FF_BRINGUP_STAGE=N in sdkconfig before building.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_1
            bool "Stage 1 - first light (panel only, no LVGL)"
            help
                IO expander up, release LCD_RST + TP_RST, backlight on,
                SPD2010 QSPI panel init, then esp_lcd_panel_draw_bitmap a
                solid fill and a two-colour split. Proves the expander,
                reset release, QSPI wiring, init sequence, RGB565 byte order
                and orientation with no GUI stack in the way.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_2
            bool "Stage 2 - the app on glass (LVGL v9, one face)"
            help
                Everything in stage 1, then LVGL v9 up via esp_lvgl_port and
                one real ff_shell face rendered (Radar, no selection) — the
                same projection the sim renders for that state.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_3
            bool "Stage 3 - touch drives it (SPD2010 -> shell input)"
            help
                Everything in stage 2, then the SPD2010 touch controller is
                wired to an LVGL pointer indev so a physical tap/swipe feeds
                the SAME abstract input events the sim ctl socket injects,
                changing shell state. On boot the stored touch calibration
                (if any) is loaded from ff_settings and applied.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_4
            bool "Stage 4 - calibrate touch (crosshair capture, then run)"
            help
                Everything in stage 3, then the S15d touch-calibration flow:
                tap five on-screen crosshairs, the per-axis affine is fitted
                (ff_touchcal_solve), the four params + all five raw->screen
                pairs are logged, and the transform is applied LIVE — boot
                continues straight into the normal UI so the corrected touch
                can be felt immediately, no reflash. NOTE: app_main still
                boots against the stub (no-op) store, so the fit is
                session-live only; bake the logged params as a compile-time
                default (or wire an NVS store) to persist across reboot.
    endchoice

    config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE
        int
        default 1 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_1
        default 2 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_2
        default 3 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_3
        default 4 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_4

    config FF_DEMO_MODE
        bool "Demo mode — boot into the fictional Firefly Fields world"
        default n
        help
            S20 demo mode (docs/specs/S20-demo-mode.md). When enabled, the
            firmware EMBED_FILES the demo festpack
            (firmware/assets/demo/firefly-fields.festpack.json) and, on
            boot, seeds the shell into "Firefly Fields" via ff_demo_seed:
            a fictional festival lineup, five seeded crew members
            (live / close-range / stale / no-fix) and a Signals feed, with
            the wall clock latched to Saturday 21:30 — so every face shows
            real-looking data with NO mesh, comms brain or real festival.

            The seeded state is genuinely computed through the real core
            APIs (honest freshness/close-range/no-fix), but the mode is a
            DEMO: it does not connect to a radio and shows a fixed,
            frozen world. Leave OFF for field builds. Meaningful at
            bring-up STAGE 2 or 3 (a face must render to see it).

    config FF_DEMO_LIVE
        bool "Live demo — advancing clock + synthetic event feed (S23)"
        depends on FF_DEMO_MODE
        default n
        help
            S23 demo-feed (docs/specs/S23-demo-feed.md). Extends S20's
            static snapshot into a LIVE world: after seeding, the demo
            clock ADVANCES from the seeded epoch by real elapsed time (so
            ages tick and scheduled events fire), and the pure-core
            ff_demofeed generator drives a reproducible stream of synthetic
            crew signals and presence pokes into the SAME ff_wiring feed
            path a real mesh uses. Incoming signals accumulate in the
            Signals feed and crew presence drifts LIVE -> STALE -> LOST and
            recovers, so the new faces have something live to build against.

            Requires FF_DEMO_MODE. When OFF (the default), FF_DEMO_MODE
            keeps S20's EXACT frozen-clock static snapshot — no generator,
            no advancing clock. Everything this switch adds is demo content,
            gated with FF_DEMO_MODE and stripped from a field build. LIVE is
            reproducible: a fixed seed + the demo epoch make the same run
            produce the same event stream (goldens use fixed snapshots, not
            wall-clock live generation — S23 AC6).

    config FF_GLASS_RULER
        bool "Draw the glass-ruler calibration pattern at boot and hold it"
        default n
        help
            Device-only diagnostic for the bezel/pixel-array offset
            investigation (docs/hardware/glass-offset.md): draws a
            boot-time "ruler" pattern (a crosshair + r=205 outer circle
            centered on the panel, four edge rulers with 2px ticks out to
            40px, four 45-degree corner markers, and dx/dy wrap-test
            striped blocks at the left and top edges) directly to the
            panel with esp_lcd_panel_draw_bitmap — no LVGL — right after
            the panel comes up, THEN HOLDS IT FOREVER (a 1s heartbeat log,
            never continuing to the boot splash, LVGL, or the normal UI)
            so the maintainer can read the offset by eye against the
            physical bezel with no time pressure.

            Default OFF: zero behaviour change to every other build. The
            drawing function itself is compiled out entirely
            (#if CONFIG_FF_GLASS_RULER) when this is off, so a field or
            demo build is byte-identical with or without this diagnostic
            existing in the tree — verify with `idf.py size` if in doubt.

    config FF_AUDIO_SELFTEST
        bool "Play FLARE_SENT once at boot (S27 sounds device bring-up aid)"
        default n
        help
            Device-only bring-up aid for the S27 sounds device HAL
            (docs/specs/S27-sounds.md, ff_audio.h): once LVGL is up and the
            first face is on glass, plays the FLARE_SENT pattern (the
            short rising 3-note chime) exactly once via ff_audio_play, so
            the maintainer can hear the speaker work without actually
            triggering a flare (which would take over the whole UI). Logs
            when it fires.

            Default OFF: zero behaviour change to every other build (no
            unsolicited sound on a normal boot). Enable, flash, listen,
            then disable again before a field build — see this spec's
            device on-glass steps for the full bring-up sequence.

endmenu
